Barnaby was not a cat who enjoyed the indoors. He was a tabby with a coat like burnt sugar and eyes the color of emeralds, and he had a restless spirit that made him pace the living room rug until his owner, Mrs. Higgins, sighed and opened the back door.

It was a Tuesday afternoon, and the rain had just stopped, leaving the garden damp and smelling of wet earth and crushed mint. Barnaby stepped onto the patio, his paws sinking slightly into the mud. He didn’t want to chase mice or hunt birds; he wanted to see what lay beyond the fence.

He walked along the edge of the garden, past the rose bushes that drooped with rain, and past the shed where the lawnmower sat. He reached the wooden fence that separated his yard from the neighbor’s. It was old, with gaps between the slats. Barnaby squeezed through one, squeezing his broad shoulders through the narrow opening. He landed on the other side in a patch of clover.

Here, the world was different. The grass was taller, and the air smelled of pine needles. He walked along the fence line, following the scent of a squirrel that had passed through earlier. The squirrel was gone, but its trail led him deeper into the woods behind the house.

Barnaby followed the trail. He passed a fallen log, stepping over it with careful, deliberate paws. He passed a stream, where the water was shallow and clear, and he drank from it, licking the cool water from his tongue. He passed a hollow tree, where he paused and looked inside, seeing nothing but darkness and the smell of old wood.

He kept walking. The woods grew darker, the trees closer together. The light from the sky filtered through the leaves in patches, like coins on the ground. He heard a bird call, sharp and bright, and he looked up, but saw only branches. He heard another sound, lower and longer, and he looked down, but saw only roots.

He came to a clearing. In the center of the clearing was a stone, flat and wide, like a table. On the stone sat a bird, a jay, blue and white, with its head turned away. Barnaby approached slowly. The bird did not move. Barnaby sat down on the edge of the stone, his tail curled around his paws. He watched the bird. The bird watched the sky.

They sat together for a long time. The sun moved across the sky, and the light changed from white to gold. The bird flew away, leaving the stone empty. Barnaby stayed on the stone. He watched the light change. He watched the shadows grow. He watched the stars come out, one by one, like small lights in the dark.

He did not go home. He stayed on the stone. He watched the night come. He watched the moon rise. He watched the stars move across the sky. He watched the dawn come, pale and gray, and the birds wake up and sing.

He stayed on the stone until the sun was high and the light was bright. Then he walked back through the woods, following the path he had made. He passed the stream, the log, the fence. He came to his own yard, where the grass was short and the roses were wet. He stepped onto the patio.

Mrs. Higgins was inside, looking out the window. She saw Barnaby on the patio, sitting on the step, his eyes closed, his tail curled around his paws. She opened the door. Barnaby walked inside. He walked to his bed, a soft cushion by the fire. He lay down. He closed his eyes.

He did not tell her where he had been. He did not tell her what he had seen. He slept. And when he woke, he paced the rug again, waiting for the door to open.
